WEBVTT

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:49.440
Theodor Steiner: So, I'm not used to doing this introduction in English because usually this podcast is in Japanese. So, bear with me if it's a lot of ums and errs, but this is the UIT Inside podcast. UIT is the front-end organization at LY Corporation, which is a Japanese company, and our major products are the LINE Messenger, which is the leading messenger in Japan, and Yahoo Japan, which is the Japanese version of the Yahoo search engine. And today I'm really excited because we have the most amazing guest ever on. We have Dan Abramov on the podcast. Dan, can you introduce yourself?

00:00:49.980 --> 00:01:06.840
Dan Abramov: Hi, thank you. My name is Dan. I guess I used to work on React, so that's how you might have seen my avatar on GitHub sometimes. And then I used to work at Bluesky. And now I'm working part-time on the Next.js team at Vercel.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.060
Theodor Steiner: And you work for Vercel Japan, is that right?

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:24.460
Dan Abramov: That's the plan. So currently I'm still in the UK, but I submitted the visa documents. So we'll see how that comes through, but the plan is to relocate to Japan, possibly at the end of the—

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.200
Theodor Steiner: That would be so cool. And you're always welcome at our office.

00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:28.880
Dan Abramov: Thank you.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:37.500
Theodor Steiner: By the way, the visa documents can take quite some time. I applied for permanent residency in Japan 16 months ago... so.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:38.530
Dan Abramov: Okay. That's—haha. Yeah.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.860
Theodor Steiner: But I hope you can get fast-tracked.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:42.670
Dan Abramov: I hope so.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:51.920
Theodor Steiner: Yeah. And obviously I had you introduce yourself, but you don't really need an introduction. When I told my colleagues that you were going to come on the show, they were all so excited.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:58.840
Theodor Steiner: So, you're not just famous in the English-speaking world, but every Japanese front-end developer also knows you.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:10.920
Dan Abramov: Oh, interesting. Because I thought people already kind of... because I disappeared a little bit from the scene. I think there's a new generation who... I haven't really talked too much.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.780
Theodor Steiner: Yeah, I guess there's a new generation as well, but you're still the legend, I guess.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:16.780
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:29.060
Theodor Steiner: So we had a meeting last Thursday, and I was like, "Yeah, Dan is coming on." And they all spammed the chat with emojis because they were so excited. And I've never had that happen with a guest coming on before.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.340
Dan Abramov: Oh, cool. Well, appreciate it.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.440
Theodor Steiner: But today we're not talking about React.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:44.180
Theodor Steiner: We're actually here to talk about something completely different, which is the ATProto protocol. So, ATProto—what does ATProto even stand for?

00:02:45.240 --> 00:03:14.300
Dan Abramov: Ah, ATProto. It's like "AT Protocol," and "AT" supposedly stands for "Authenticated Transfer." So I think that's kind of the formal name. I think really the reason is because it looks like the @ symbol that you use in usernames, and it's a protocol that has to do with identity. So it's kind of like when you say my Instagram handle is @whatever, except it's an internet protocol instead of something specific to a company.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:29.600
Theodor Steiner: Okay. So you and I are both on Bluesky, so I think we have quite a bit of contact with the AT Protocol. But the majority of Japanese developers are still stuck on Twitter or X, and they might have never heard of AT Protocol.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:29.940
Dan Abramov: Mhm.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:36.560
Theodor Steiner: Can you just give us the 100-foot bird's-eye view of the protocol? What is that?

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:54.880
Dan Abramov: Yeah, it's very tricky to answer because a good protocol stays invisible. It's a way to build social apps—not necessarily social, but I guess that's an easier area to focus on.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:15.580
Dan Abramov: It's a new way to build social apps that was originally developed by Bluesky, the company that you might know as a Twitter clone product. But really, originally they were supposed to do that with Twitter itself. It's just that Twitter broke the contract with them.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:29.480
Dan Abramov: But the thing they originally wanted to build is a generic, universal internet protocol. I've tried to describe it in different ways. I don't know which one resonates. Different audiences understand it differently.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:52.720
Dan Abramov: One way I would describe it to web developers is that it tries to extract an identity layer for the web. So each app has its own login, and for social apps you can create different kinds of things in the app, but they stay within that app, right?

00:04:52.960 --> 00:05:08.260
Dan Abramov: Like if I write an answer on Stack Overflow, it's kind of stuck inside Stack Overflow. If I write a comment on GitHub, it's kind of stuck inside GitHub, in the sense that these companies own what I made using their products.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:22.660
Dan Abramov: And then if these companies start changing in ways that you don't like, maybe you can abandon them, but you've invested so much effort and so much content into these closed spaces that they control.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:32.300
Dan Abramov: And the idea of AT Protocol, or shorter, ATProto, is to kind of lift that out so that everything works more like files.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:51.980
Dan Abramov: So when I post on Bluesky, I'm not actually putting stuff into the Bluesky app. Although it looks like this, right? If you just use it, it kind of looks like Twitter. Or if you use Tangled, which is ATProto's GitHub alternative, it kind of looks like GitHub.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:06:01.660
Dan Abramov: But what happens behind the scenes is actually it's almost like I have a folder somewhere in the cloud that has data from all of these apps as JSON.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:20.540
Dan Abramov: So everything is stored as JSON in this new layer of the web that is app agnostic. It's not controlled by one company necessarily, right? You can host this folder with different providers. You can host it yourself. It is really cheap.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:37.140
Dan Abramov: But it's app agnostic, and as a result anyone can start a competitor to one of these services or one of these apps with all existing data already there and already interoperable with existing apps.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:53.640
Dan Abramov: So if somebody wants to start a competitor to Bluesky—and this actually happened—there's Blacksky, which has different moderation policies and so on, but it's completely interoperable with the Bluesky app even though it doesn't use the Bluesky API.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:07:11.320
Dan Abramov: So that is the big thing. Every app on ATProto shares a common API where you don't need to provide an API as the app author because all apps just write to this JSON.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:28.320
Dan Abramov: It's a common way for apps to, in a sense, talk to each other—or show content from each other and augment each other—without actually talking to each other. So without exposing an API, because the protocol itself is the API.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:54.260
Dan Abramov: There's a few other ways I like to explain it, but I think that's the big idea: freeing the stuff that we create, lifting it out of apps, and making it live on an independent layer that we actually control as users, so that everything we create is more like files under the hood, but with the same normal user experience for the app user.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:08:08.320
Theodor Steiner: And you actually have a great blog post on this. You have "A Social File System" on your blog, Overreacted, and it talks about what you just did the perfect summary of.

00:08:08.413 --> 00:08:08.440
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.200
Theodor Steiner: Actually, we can just end the podcast here.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:21.600
Theodor Steiner: But I think we want to dive a bit deeper, and since this podcast is mostly for web developers, I actually prepared a little ambush for you.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:42.720
Theodor Steiner: So, ATProto is a protocol, and web developers are usually familiar with the HTTP protocol. So I sort of wanted to go up the stack layer by layer and see where they differ, what ATProto offers on top of HTTP, and how they even build on each other.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:43.240
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:51.240
Theodor Steiner: And this might feel like a check of your HTTP knowledge, but we'll keep it real simple and easy.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:09:10.020
Theodor Steiner: So, layer zero of HTTP is: what is HTTP even for? It would be a communication standard between clients and servers. And you already hinted at this, but what is layer zero behind the ATProto protocol?

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:26.320
Dan Abramov: Yeah. ATProto kind of builds on top of multiple things. It builds on top of HTTP, DNS, JSON, WebSockets, CBOR, and I think maybe these are the ones that I know.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:40.120
Dan Abramov: I'm sure there are other things, but it's HTTP or WebSockets as the transport, and then CBOR as encoding, I think, for JSON, and then DNS for a bunch of...

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:45.960
Theodor Steiner: So it doesn't really try to reinvent anything about how the internet works.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:46.280
Dan Abramov: Right.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:50.580
Theodor Steiner: It sort of builds on top of the existing transport layer of the internet.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:10:18.220
Dan Abramov: Yeah. And I guess it has a few of its own inventions. There's something called Lexicon, which is a JSON type definition. They did not go with RDF or JSON-LD or JSON Schema. They made their own because none of these things quite fit what they wanted.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:25.800
Dan Abramov: So there are some custom pieces, but where appropriate it tries to reuse existing primitives.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:54.060
Dan Abramov: For example, domains as handles is one example. Instead of inventing its own handle system, it's like, well, it has to be centralized in some sense, because somebody has to determine the top-level mappings, like who owns this username. And they decided, well, this already exists on the internet. It's called domains. So we're just going to use this existing system for this.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:57.740
Dan Abramov: I think there's a lot of that in the design.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:17.060
Dan Abramov: And then there's also cryptography. In addition to all of this stuff, there's cryptography—not in the sense of crypto, blockchain, or whatever, even though there is one element that's maybe reminiscent of that. We can get to that if we want to.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:26.060
Dan Abramov: But overall it's much more like the web. There are cryptographic signatures and public-key/private-key cryptography.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:28.200
Theodor Steiner: The math part of cryptography.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:28.920
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:29.390
Theodor Steiner: Yeah.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:29.950
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:48.340
Theodor Steiner: So whereas HTTP—HTTP and ATProto both have URIs. For HTTP, everyone in the web world knows URLs. You have a domain that maps to a server, and DNS helps resolve the path to the server.

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:54.100
Theodor Steiner: But in the ATProto world, what is the common URI? What is in there?

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:56.620
Dan Abramov: So in ATProto, all AT URIs...

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:09.760
Dan Abramov: First, it's worth talking about the AT URI itself. It's `at://`, and then what goes after it?

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:13.660
Dan Abramov: I think the first question is: what does it point to?

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:24.500
Dan Abramov: An HTTP URI points at a song, an image, an HTML page, or something like this. Maybe that HTML page has more links.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:33.900
Dan Abramov: In ATProto, all AT URIs point at records. And a record is a piece of JSON with a typed schema.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.020
Dan Abramov: Essentially, every AT URI points at some JSON.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:13:00.480
Dan Abramov: For example, a Bluesky post is a piece of JSON. Or Tangled—the GitHub alternative—a star is also a piece of JSON. A Bluesky follow is a piece of JSON that says you follow this person.

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:06.920
Dan Abramov: So it's the stuff that you create: these documents, or records, through the apps.

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.320
Dan Abramov: These records can link to each other.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:26.300
Dan Abramov: For example, if I comment on your post, each of us has a folder, or a repo, where all of our stuff lives. The important part is that you have yours and I have mine.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.360
Dan Abramov: Each of us owns our own data.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:44.560
Dan Abramov: Sure, maybe we use some hosting behind the scenes. When I sign up for Bluesky, I don't think about where it lives. But I actually moved my hosting, so you can move it over time.

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:49.180
Dan Abramov: All my stuff is in my repo, and all your stuff is in your repo.

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:57.240
Dan Abramov: Actually, I'm going to give you a test to see if you understand it.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:02.300
Dan Abramov: If 10,000 people follow you, where do these follow records live?

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:05.320
Dan Abramov: Are they in your repo, or are they somewhere else?

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:10.540
Theodor Steiner: I would say they are distributed across 10,000 users, right?

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:10.900
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:12.900
Theodor Steiner: Because everyone holds their own follow.

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:14.320
Dan Abramov: Yeah, exactly.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:23.340
Dan Abramov: So if you have a million followers, they're not actually yours. There's one follow record per person who follows you.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:31.140
Dan Abramov: But if you follow a million people, that's a million records in your repo, because it was your choice to follow them.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:45.700
Dan Abramov: So now that we understand your stuff lives in your repo and my stuff lives in mine, if I comment on your posts, that's a comment record in my repo that has a link to posts in your repo.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:52.840
Dan Abramov: And that's where AT URIs come in. I need a way to link to something that exists in your repo—to some piece of JSON.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:15:00.360
Dan Abramov: So we have this web of JSON where, instead of HTML pages with links, we have JSON with links.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:05.240
Dan Abramov: That's where the need for a separate protocol comes up.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:24.880
Dan Abramov: If we just used HTTP, imagine there's some server hosting your data at `hosting-company.com/you/post`, and I link to that from my repo. That means you can never change the hosting company without breaking the link.

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:48.540
Dan Abramov: We have the domain system, which lets whoever hosts the content change where it's hosted—change the IP address—but it doesn't let you move away from that hosting company without breaking the links, because it's still hosted by `hosting-company.com`.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:58.720
Dan Abramov: From a product-design perspective, that is the big reason why everything went wrong.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:10.540
Dan Abramov: We put stuff into these apps, into these boxes, and then it stays there because they have power over us.

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:20.780
Dan Abramov: So coming back to your question: what is in the AT Protocol link?

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.780
Dan Abramov: It also starts with a domain authority.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:27.460
Dan Abramov: You may see something like `at://dabra.ms/...`.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:45.820
Dan Abramov: But the important part is that the first thing is not where it's hosted.

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:53.700
Dan Abramov: It's not the physical server. It's not the domain for the physical server. It's my username, in a sense.

00:16:54.300 --> 00:17:02.320
Dan Abramov: So there is an extra resolution step where we find out, okay, for this username, where is the current server where this is hosted?

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:05.960
Dan Abramov: Then it goes to HTTP to fetch it.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:14.400
Dan Abramov: So it's one indirection that puts usernames before the physical storage.

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:20.180
Dan Abramov: That lets me change where my actual hosting is over time, and none of the links will break.

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:23.500
Dan Abramov: That's the big motivation for why it's done this way.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:39.900
Theodor Steiner: So if I rephrase this in my own words: the issue with HTTP is that it uses URLs, and URLs point at locations. The issue is that those locations can change—they're sort of ephemeral.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:52.260
Theodor Steiner: But ATProto says we somehow need this to never change. We need some resource identifier that will always point at your repo.

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:53.290
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:57.000
Dan Abramov: Or you could say that it literally puts people first.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:05.400
Dan Abramov: In a URL, the authority part is the physical host of the location.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:16.920
Dan Abramov: In an AT URI—and there's a whole tangent about whether it should technically be called a URI—but essentially the authority part is the user themselves.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:22.760
Dan Abramov: So it's not like `bluesky.com/danabra...`; it's `dabra.ms/...`.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.620
Dan Abramov: It inverts the relationship so that the user comes first.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.900
Dan Abramov: There's one nuance I wanted to flag.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:37.920
Dan Abramov: When we talk about the username, that's the friendly way to write an AT URI.

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:42.460
Dan Abramov: But it's actually unstable because you can change your username over time.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:53.120
Dan Abramov: The full form of the URI starts with something called a DID, which is a permanent identifier that never changes.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:59.180
Dan Abramov: So this is the thing that's forever immutable, as long as you're in control of your identity.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:06.880
Theodor Steiner: Okay, I want to come back to the DID part because I think it's really important. I didn't know it was pronounced "did"—no pun intended.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:29.860
Theodor Steiner: So to get this back to the HTTP discussion: in HTTP you have a domain. You could perhaps lose access to your domain, and the domain sort of allows you to obfuscate where your data actually lives. But you could lose the domain, and that's the issue.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:46.020
Theodor Steiner: And to solve that, the AT Protocol adds a different layer that says, "No, the domain, or your server, is not what we're looking for. The identity is what we're looking for. And if we have your identity, we can resolve that to where you host in HTTP."

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:52.100
Dan Abramov: Yes. I wouldn't say it's because you can lose it, although certainly you can lose a domain.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:20:02.000
Dan Abramov: I think it's more about... if we're talking about something like a tweet, it's not going to live on your domain anyway. It lives on Twitter.

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:13.100
Dan Abramov: So if we try to design this thing with HTTP, then the HTTP URI would be something like `twitter.com` or `twitter-data-storage.com`, or whatever.

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:23.780
Dan Abramov: But then the problem is you can't walk away from Twitter and use a different app. You can't get rid of Twitter without breaking all these links.

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:42.880
Dan Abramov: It's more about how, when each of us had our own site, we were directly connected to how the internet works. If we don't like the hosting, we just change our hosting. Our users don't need to know—it still resolves.

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:54.400
Dan Abramov: But when we moved into social spaces like Facebook, Twitter, or Stack Overflow, we're now one step disconnected from how the internet actually works because we can't change our hosting.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:21:01.320
Dan Abramov: You can't change your hosting when you're using Twitter. Twitter couples the app and the hosting.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:18.600
Dan Abramov: And so the idea here is: let's still make your content live in a different world where you can change the hosting, it's app agnostic, and the app is just one of the consumers of this layer of the internet.

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:23.800
Dan Abramov: In that sense, I like to think about it as similar to RSS and Google Reader.

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:35.920
Dan Abramov: You control your RSS. You can move where you host it, and then you can consume all of the RSS you're interested in from apps such as Google Reader.

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:45.720
Dan Abramov: But they actually have no sway on the underlying system. If Google Reader disappears, somebody else can make an RSS client that reads from all of them.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:54.420
Dan Abramov: So it's really about avoiding lock-in. It's about removing the power from the apps to keep you locked down.

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:56.860
Theodor Steiner: That's a really nice way of putting it.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:04.860
Theodor Steiner: And now that we know the promise of the AT Protocol, I kind of want to dive into how it works.

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:08.700
Theodor Steiner: So you said there's this DID that can be resolved to your identity.

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:14.420
Theodor Steiner: For HTTP, the domain would be resolved to your location via DNS.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.640
Theodor Steiner: You do a DNS lookup and you get the IP address.

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:21.720
Theodor Steiner: How is the DID resolved to you?

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.820
Dan Abramov: Yeah. So let's first start with what a DID is.

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:31.740
Dan Abramov: This is a W3C standard. They tried to reuse this part.

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:38.640
Dan Abramov: It's kind of a meta-standard in the sense that it doesn't actually specify how to resolve a DID.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:46.760
Dan Abramov: It's more like a DID is a format where first it says `did:`, and then there's something called the DID method.

00:22:47.720 --> 00:23:00.700
Dan Abramov: This can be three letters—or maybe more—but the method specifies how to find a certain thing: a piece of JSON that's your identity.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:06.700
Dan Abramov: From that point, the rest is just an identifier for this method.

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:15.740
Dan Abramov: So it's almost like DID is just a universal umbrella for many identity systems.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:23.860
Dan Abramov: ATProto picks two DID systems supported by the DID standard because they satisfy certain properties.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:29.580
Dan Abramov: One property is that they have to be fast to resolve.

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:35.180
Dan Abramov: Something like "download the whole blockchain" is already a non-starter.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:38.440
Dan Abramov: It has to be something that's easily resolvable.

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:44.120
Dan Abramov: I don't remember the exact properties, but they basically picked two things.

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:46.120
Dan Abramov: One is the one they invented.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:49.060
Dan Abramov: There are two DID methods supported by ATProto.

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:58.600
Dan Abramov: One is called `did:plc`, which initially stood for "placeholder" because they thought they were going to replace it. Then they decided, "Actually, it's quite good. We're going to keep it."

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:02.900
Dan Abramov: Now it's supposedly "Public Ledger of Credentials."

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:06.080
Theodor Steiner: Okay, that's a crypto-like ledger.

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:10.680
Dan Abramov: Yeah. I think that is an influence from that world.

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:13.820
Dan Abramov: But it's actually not distributed.

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:15.520
Dan Abramov: This is the centralized part.

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:17.020
Dan Abramov: It's kind of like an npm registry.

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:22.780
Dan Abramov: The other DID method is `did:web`, which uses a normal domain lookup.

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:25.680
Dan Abramov: So these are two ways this can work.

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:33.620
Dan Abramov: In both cases, the goal is to resolve to a piece of JSON that is called your DID document, which I kind of like to think of as a passport.

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:36.720
Dan Abramov: It's a piece of JSON that says three things.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:42.140
Dan Abramov: One is where your stuff currently lives—your HTTP hosting.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:47.860
Dan Abramov: Another is your handle—your domain username.

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:50.100
Dan Abramov: It can be completely different from where it's hosted.

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:54.300
Dan Abramov: The third thing is your public key.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:25:00.100
Dan Abramov: So if somebody wants to verify some record, what is the public key that they verify against?

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:02.420
Dan Abramov: These are the two methods.

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:05.500
Dan Abramov: The difference between them is that one is more centralized.

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:10.540
Dan Abramov: Legally, they're spinning it off into an independent Swiss organization.

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:24.220
Dan Abramov: The goal is for it to evolve similarly to how the root DNS has evolved with ICANN, with some neutral organization managing it.

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:35.440
Dan Abramov: Being centralized is kind of the point, because that is the authoritative place to say what's under this identity.

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:40.720
Dan Abramov: But they came up with a bunch of things that make it less of a problem that it's centralized.

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:43.840
Dan Abramov: For example, it's entirely open source.

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:45.680
Dan Abramov: There is no private state in it.

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:49.800
Dan Abramov: It's fully replayable based on public data.

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:51.500
Dan Abramov: You can audit it.

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:52.750
Dan Abramov: There's an audit log.

00:25:52.750 --> 00:25:54.330
Dan Abramov: You can inspect every transaction.

00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:56.150
Dan Abramov: It's highly auditable.

00:25:58.730 --> 00:26:00.050
Dan Abramov: That's the usual way.

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:01.810
Dan Abramov: If you sign up on Bluesky, you're going to get a `did:plc` identity.

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:06.730
Dan Abramov: PLC is the thing that holds your DID document.

00:26:06.970 --> 00:26:17.630
Dan Abramov: But if you hate the idea of anything centralized, there's `did:web`, where the identifier is literally a domain instead of a hash.

00:26:19.490 --> 00:26:20.270
Dan Abramov: With `did:plc` you get a hash.

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:21.320
Dan Abramov: With `did:web` you get a domain.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:26.410
Dan Abramov: But if you lose the domain, you can't control your identity anymore—it effectively dies.

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:30.670
Dan Abramov: So that's kind of the choice for hardcore decentralizers.

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:38.730
Theodor Steiner: So the PLC directory is a centralized place to look up DIDs that use the PLC method.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:45.310
Theodor Steiner: And "Public Ledger" sort of implies that there's a way I can look up the ledger.

00:26:47.590 --> 00:26:53.810
Theodor Steiner: I can see what happened on the ledger, and "public" means probably everyone can look it up.

00:26:53.870 --> 00:26:58.830
Theodor Steiner: Can I just do an HTTP request somewhere and get information about my ID?

00:26:58.830 --> 00:26:59.280
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:05.410
Dan Abramov: There is something called the PLC directory.

00:27:05.410 --> 00:27:06.190
Dan Abramov: It's a website.

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:10.790
Dan Abramov: It has an example where DID resolution can be as simple as:

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:11.510
Dan Abramov: `curl https://plc.directory/<did>`

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:17.310
Dan Abramov: and that gives you your JSON.

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:20.270
Dan Abramov: So there's an API.

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:25.030
Dan Abramov: There's also an API for retrieving past versions.

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:31.630
Dan Abramov: You can see, for every DID document, what it says now and what it said in the past.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:40.370
Dan Abramov: You can verify that each change is signed by the public key from the previous version of the DID document.

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:50.490
Dan Abramov: So you can independently verify that the DID document could not have been tampered with, because it's signed by the user's private key, which the PLC directory doesn't have.

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:59.910
Dan Abramov: And then the first version of the DID document, when hashed, that hash is your identifier on PLC.

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:06.210
Dan Abramov: So you can verify that the first version is also correct because it has the signature.

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:09.970
Dan Abramov: It's a hash of the signed version of the first document.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:22.550
Dan Abramov: So you can recursively verify, for every user, the entire chain of changes to their identity, including changes to hosting, the key pair, and the username.

00:28:23.250 --> 00:28:36.430
Theodor Steiner: So it's a cryptographically verifiable chain of events of what happened to your passport, so to say, with no loose ends, because the first entry is the DID itself—the identifier itself.

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:37.370
Dan Abramov: Yes.

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:37.650
Theodor Steiner: Okay.

00:28:37.660 --> 00:28:37.660
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:28:37.670 --> 00:28:38.150
Theodor Steiner: Very cool.

00:28:38.150 --> 00:28:41.790
Theodor Steiner: And you said there are three parts living in there.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:41.890
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:48.630
Theodor Steiner: There's your alias, there's where your data lives...

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:48.950
Dan Abramov: Mhm.

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:50.310
Theodor Steiner: ...and then what was the last part?

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:51.650
Dan Abramov: The public key.

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:53.170
Theodor Steiner: The public key. Okay.

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:57.350
Theodor Steiner: So people can verify that it's actually what it claims to be.

00:28:58.030 --> 00:29:01.130
Theodor Steiner: So where does the data actually live?

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:03.050
Theodor Steiner: What would be in my DID document?

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:07.030
Dan Abramov: So it depends on how you made your ATProto account.

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:13.750
Dan Abramov: We call this layer above the web the "atmosphere."

00:29:16.270 --> 00:29:23.770
Dan Abramov: So having an atmosphere account is kind of like having a Google account, except it's the web.

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:38.730
Dan Abramov: I think that's part of the idea—that we all just have these web accounts instead of many accounts tied to a specific provider like Google.

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:43.610
Dan Abramov: If you follow the DID document, it will say...

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:54.690
Dan Abramov: For example, if I sign up for ATProto through the Bluesky app, it has to create hosting for me because I don't already have ATProto hosting.

00:29:57.230 --> 00:29:58.330
Dan Abramov: Bluesky is actually two different things.

00:29:58.770 --> 00:30:04.710
Dan Abramov: There's Bluesky hosting under the hood, and there's the Bluesky app. They're completely independent.

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:08.170
Dan Abramov: I actually changed my hosting to a different provider.

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:13.190
Dan Abramov: You can also self-host. You can run a server yourself. It's really cheap.

00:30:16.150 --> 00:30:22.590
Dan Abramov: If you sign up through Bluesky, it's going to point to something like `*.host.bsky.network` or whatever.

00:30:23.350 --> 00:30:30.950
Dan Abramov: This is the server that hosts your JSON, contains your private key, and is able to serve your data.

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:41.970
Dan Abramov: If apps want to know what you've posted, it can serve your posts and all the other information from other atmosphere apps.

00:30:42.890 --> 00:30:47.710
Dan Abramov: It also provides a WebSocket connection for notifying about changes.

00:30:48.370 --> 00:30:50.350
Dan Abramov: That's why this whole thing works in real time.

00:30:50.870 --> 00:30:53.370
Dan Abramov: It's not just pull.

00:30:55.710 --> 00:31:04.950
Dan Abramov: Apps subscribe to your hosting, and they're notified when something changes so they can immediately ingest it and put it into their databases.

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:11.410
Theodor Steiner: So for the traditional web, this would be, for example, the Twitter server.

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:15.810
Theodor Steiner: I want to post something to Twitter, and it goes to the Twitter server.

00:31:16.970 --> 00:31:22.690
Theodor Steiner: But in ATProto, this is the PDS part, right? The Personal Data Server.

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:24.200
Dan Abramov: Yeah. Yes.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:28.190
Theodor Steiner: And my data only lives on one server.

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:32.930
Theodor Steiner: So it's like a one-to-one mapping between my DID document and the PDS.

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:37.310
Dan Abramov: Yeah. Your data always points to one.

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:41.290
Dan Abramov: I mean, one server can serve many users' data, right?

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:46.050
Dan Abramov: It scales, but your own stuff only lives in one place.

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:47.710
Dan Abramov: At least the source of truth.

00:31:50.490 --> 00:32:00.270
Dan Abramov: Of course, apps consume it and have their own local caches or databases, but the source of truth is your PDS.

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:03.730
Theodor Steiner: So I think I'm actually on the same PDS as you.

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:05.690
Theodor Steiner: I'm German, and I moved to eurosky.social.

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:07.010
Dan Abramov: Mhm. Yeah.

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:08.950
Theodor Steiner: I think that's the PDS.

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:09.270
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:11.130
Theodor Steiner: And I did a migration and everything.

00:32:11.350 --> 00:32:11.550
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:32:11.570 --> 00:32:14.250
Theodor Steiner: I think that's the interesting part you talked about earlier.

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:23.410
Theodor Steiner: Since your identity stays in one place, you can switch where your data lives and everything follows it around.

00:32:23.410 --> 00:32:26.570
Theodor Steiner: So I had a Bluesky account.

00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:33.150
Theodor Steiner: Initially, signing up left me with the default Bluesky PDS.

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:39.210
Theodor Steiner: Then I made a bunch of posts, a bunch of likes, followed, of course, Dan Abramov on Bluesky...

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:43.410
Theodor Steiner: And then at some point I wanted to move that to Europe.

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:48.370
Theodor Steiner: What happened behind the scenes when I did the migration?

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:49.260
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:53.150
Dan Abramov: The first thing that happened is that your entire repository—all of your JSON—was copied.

00:32:58.250 --> 00:33:01.310
Dan Abramov: If you're familiar with Git, it's kind of like pushing Git to a different remote.

00:33:04.030 --> 00:33:08.250
Dan Abramov: Your repository has been pushed to another place.

00:33:12.050 --> 00:33:14.470
Dan Abramov: You also had to migrate your media.

00:33:14.770 --> 00:33:16.090
Dan Abramov: Currently that's a little wonky.

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:19.890
Dan Abramov: You have to use a separate tool to migrate all the image files.

00:33:21.970 --> 00:33:25.970
Dan Abramov: I think they're planning to improve this so the migration goes server-to-server.

00:33:28.010 --> 00:33:31.670
Dan Abramov: Currently it goes server-to-client-to-server, which is why it's a little strange.

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:36.290
Dan Abramov: Ideally it would be much faster and a smoother process.

00:33:37.270 --> 00:33:44.810
Dan Abramov: Once that repository appears on the other server, the last thing that happens is you submit a change to the PLC directory.

00:33:47.090 --> 00:33:54.070
Dan Abramov: There's a request that says, "Update my passport—my DID document—to say this is now my hosting."

00:33:54.070 --> 00:33:58.950
Dan Abramov: This is now the HTTP endpoint where my content actually lives.

00:34:00.650 --> 00:34:01.100
Dan Abramov: That's the last step.

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:06.510
Dan Abramov: That change is then broadcast from the PLC directory.

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:21.450
Dan Abramov: I think the dominant model is that apps cache identities for something like five minutes or an hour.

00:34:22.750 --> 00:34:29.370
Dan Abramov: So the next time they resolve your DID they'll see, "Okay, the content is hosted somewhere else."

00:34:29.530 --> 00:34:36.350
Dan Abramov: But for most apps they don't even need to do anything because they've already cached all the data by its AT URI.

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:38.770
Dan Abramov: The AT URIs didn't change.

00:34:41.970 --> 00:34:45.430
Dan Abramov: If they ever need to refetch something, they can simply go to your new server.

00:34:46.470 --> 00:34:51.490
Dan Abramov: So for apps it barely matters because most apps just cache the data.

00:34:52.570 --> 00:34:53.770
Theodor Steiner: So basically what I did was...

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:56.090
Theodor Steiner: I had a repo with all my data.

00:34:57.070 --> 00:34:57.520
Theodor Steiner: It was on GitHub.

00:34:58.470 --> 00:35:08.550
Theodor Steiner: Then I did a Git push to GitLab instead, deleted the old repo on GitHub, and updated my passport to say, "Hey, my data now lives over here."

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:10.460
Dan Abramov: Yeah. That's basically how it works.

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:21.790
Theodor Steiner: And in this repo—you said there's a bunch of JSON living in it—but what kinds of JSON are there?

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:25.230
Dan Abramov: There's actually a great tool to inspect it.

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:26.340
Dan Abramov: It's called **pds.ls**.

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:40.410
Dan Abramov: If you literally type "PDS" as in Personal Data Server, and "ls" as in the Unix `ls` utility for listing files, it'll give you a page where you can input any ATProto handle.

00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:48.430
Dan Abramov: You can input your domain if you have an ATProto account associated with it, and it'll show you the whole repository.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:54.370
Dan Abramov: You'll see collections, which are kind of like folders.

00:35:55.230 --> 00:36:02.110
Dan Abramov: If you search for yourself, it'll resolve the DID, resolve the host, and you'll see it says **eurosky.social**.

00:36:03.510 --> 00:36:06.410
Dan Abramov: So it resolves where your hosting actually lives.

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:09.630
Dan Abramov: Then these collections are kind of like folders.

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:11.990
Dan Abramov: They're grouped by type.

00:36:12.550 --> 00:36:16.250
Dan Abramov: These are all different types of JSON.

00:36:17.070 --> 00:36:17.520
Dan Abramov: They're app-specific.

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:21.070
Dan Abramov: The Bluesky app has its own types.

00:36:22.130 --> 00:36:25.150
Dan Abramov: npmx has its own types.

00:36:26.150 --> 00:36:28.150
Dan Abramov: Every app developer can come up with their own.

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:31.610
Dan Abramov: Nobody is policing them.

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:38.750
Dan Abramov: They're sorted by reverse domain, which naturally gives them unique prefixes.

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:43.010
Dan Abramov: If you work on some app, you just put your app's domain there.

00:36:43.010 --> 00:36:44.610
Dan Abramov: It's kind of like a namespace.

00:36:45.430 --> 00:36:46.790
Dan Abramov: Almost like scoped packages on npm.

00:36:47.210 --> 00:36:55.790
Dan Abramov: And then if you go into any folder, you're going to see a list of JSON files, or records, in that folder that all have the same type.

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:58.310
Dan Abramov: And it's just normal JSON.

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:05.750
Dan Abramov: A like would be a JSON object that says which post was liked, for example.

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:07.650
Dan Abramov: These are going to be links.

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:15.570
Dan Abramov: So if you click into a like, or any specific piece of JSON, there's going to be a specific...

00:37:16.090 --> 00:37:25.770
Dan Abramov: For example, this is a like for a certain post. And if you follow the link to the post, you might end up on a different person's repository with the actual post.

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:42.390
Theodor Steiner: Okay, so for the people listening to this podcast, I just navigated to **pds.ls**, and then I put my alias—so my domain, **yoshana.de**—on there and sort of did the DID lookup.

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:43.450
Theodor Steiner: Is that what you call it?

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:43.670
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:37:43.690 --> 00:37:44.590
Theodor Steiner: The PLC lookup.

00:37:44.830 --> 00:37:47.910
Theodor Steiner: And it found my PDS, which in this case is **eurosky.social**.

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:55.690
Theodor Steiner: Below that, we had a bunch of records—no, wait, a bunch of collections.

00:37:56.890 --> 00:38:05.070
Theodor Steiner: The collections are all of a certain type, I'd say. And the type is a reverse-domain identifier.

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:08.590
Theodor Steiner: So for Bluesky likes it is `app.bsky.feed.like`.

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:13.750
Theodor Steiner: If I click on that, I get all the likes I ever did on Bluesky.

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:18.870
Theodor Steiner: And if I click even deeper into that, I get the actual JSON.

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:28.250
Theodor Steiner: Here it says it has a timestamp, or `createdAt`, and then it has a subject, which I assume is the target of my like.

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:30.030
Dan Abramov: That's the post.

00:38:30.690 --> 00:38:33.290
Theodor Steiner: Why does it have a URI and a CID?

00:38:33.316 --> 00:38:33.450
Dan Abramov: Yeah, that's the post you liked.

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:35.610
Theodor Steiner: So URI is the thing we talked about earlier.

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:47.510
Theodor Steiner: This will resolve to somebody else's DID document, and from there we can get the PDS, and from there we can get the collection, and from there we can get the entry—the post in the collection.

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:48.970
Theodor Steiner: But what is the CID here?

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:53.470
Dan Abramov: Yeah. So there are two types of links in ATProto.

00:38:54.150 --> 00:39:01.610
Dan Abramov: One is a weak link, or I think it's called the ref, which is just a normal AT URI.

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:07.690
Dan Abramov: And then another one is called a strong link, which is URI and CID.

00:39:08.530 --> 00:39:11.670
Dan Abramov: CID is just a hash of the linked record.

00:39:12.410 --> 00:39:22.850
Dan Abramov: The idea is that for some links, you maybe want apps to be able to tell: is the thing that it relates to the same as it was?

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:28.310
Dan Abramov: For example, for the like, if the post was edited, does that still count?

00:39:28.790 --> 00:39:35.710
Dan Abramov: I think the Bluesky app actually doesn't... I don't know. I don't think it reflects edits. I'm not sure. Maybe it doesn't.

00:39:35.710 --> 00:39:41.410
Dan Abramov: So this is an application choice to disregard edits, or it's a product decision.

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:55.450
Dan Abramov: But if they ever allowed edits, this would allow you to say, "Was your like on the version that's there now, or has it changed since the like?"

00:39:55.990 --> 00:40:16.670
Theodor Steiner: Okay. So if, for example, I liked a post that said tabs are better than spaces, and then at some point the poster—the author of the post—went back and said spaces are better than tabs, I can point at this record and say no, because the hash changed. Back when I liked it, it was a different post.

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:16.990
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:17.240
Theodor Steiner: Okay.

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:17.470
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:21.390
Theodor Steiner: And what is the `$type` field here in the JSON?

00:40:21.570 --> 00:40:24.110
Dan Abramov: Yeah. So this is the name of the collection it's in.

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:28.150
Dan Abramov: It's a convention that it's always going to be there.

00:40:28.150 --> 00:40:35.490
Dan Abramov: So it lets you tell just from the record what kind of record it is without even knowing the URI.

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:37.810
Dan Abramov: It's just the folder name, basically.

00:40:39.050 --> 00:40:44.950
Theodor Steiner: So a like would have a very different data structure to, let's say, a post.

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:50.090
Theodor Steiner: How does Bluesky know how to parse those records?

00:40:50.770 --> 00:40:52.490
Dan Abramov: Yeah. So this is an interesting question.

00:40:56.810 --> 00:40:59.550
Dan Abramov: There are these things called lexicons, which are kind of like type definitions.

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:02.390
Dan Abramov: I really think of them as a lexicon.

00:41:03.070 --> 00:41:16.230
Dan Abramov: For each of these types—Bluesky like, Bluesky post, Tangled repo, npmx follow, whatever—each of these things has a schema associated with it, written as JSON.

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:19.470
Dan Abramov: The funny thing is, you don't have to publish them.

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:36.230
Dan Abramov: They're useful for apps because there's tooling that generates actual parsing code and validation, or Zod validation, so that you can safely read it into a typed form in any language.

00:41:36.610 --> 00:41:40.670
Dan Abramov: There's JavaScript, Swift, Go, and so on.

00:41:42.970 --> 00:41:45.010
Dan Abramov: There are codegen libraries that do this from lexicons.

00:41:46.670 --> 00:41:52.950
Dan Abramov: But you can also publish lexicons, and the funny thing is the way you publish them is they're also records.

00:41:53.045 --> 00:41:53.310
Theodor Steiner: Mhm. Of course.

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:57.490
Dan Abramov: So you can publish a record with the type definition.

00:41:57.930 --> 00:42:08.030
Dan Abramov: And actually, in **pds.ls**, if the type is published, you can click on it as a link and it will show the record.

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:25.130
Dan Abramov: So on the screen you were showing, where you had the like record and it says `$type: app.bsky.feed.like`, if you press on it, there's a lexicon resolution mechanism which says how to find the record that has the schema for this type.

00:42:25.610 --> 00:42:30.410
Dan Abramov: And **pds.ls** actually displays it nicely, almost like documentation.

00:42:31.870 --> 00:42:32.320
Theodor Steiner: That's pretty cool.

00:42:32.630 --> 00:42:42.650
Theodor Steiner: So this is how they allow storing arbitrary data, because there's a way to define how data is structured, and every app gets to define this themselves.

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:43.580
Dan Abramov: Yes.

00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:45.810
Dan Abramov: Although it's even more interesting.

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:51.530
Dan Abramov: I think that is the starting point: as a developer, you choose your own formats.

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:54.170
Dan Abramov: But you can also choose to reuse existing ones.

00:42:55.590 --> 00:43:04.230
Dan Abramov: You can decide that you're making, maybe, a plugin for Bluesky and then use Bluesky follows as a format for your thing.

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:12.190
Dan Abramov: Or maybe you're making some kind of annotation, like a Community Notes feature or something like this.

00:43:14.710 --> 00:43:16.990
Dan Abramov: In that case, maybe it would make sense for Bluesky to do it.

00:43:16.990 --> 00:43:31.750
Dan Abramov: But maybe you're making some kind of annotation thing, and then you could allow the target of your annotation to be Bluesky posts, Tangled repos, or anything. You can link to anything.

00:43:32.050 --> 00:43:36.030
Dan Abramov: So in that sense, you can use other apps' formats.

00:43:36.030 --> 00:43:41.130
Dan Abramov: You can link to them, or you can literally use them as your own format.

00:43:42.050 --> 00:43:44.530
Dan Abramov: It's the same as with file formats, right?

00:43:45.070 --> 00:43:46.910
Dan Abramov: Many apps use the same format.

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:50.690
Dan Abramov: There's actually been precedent.

00:43:51.250 --> 00:43:54.150
Dan Abramov: There are three blogging platforms on ATProto.

00:43:54.550 --> 00:44:00.750
Dan Abramov: One is called Leaflet, another Offprint, and another is called pckt.blog.

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:14.330
Dan Abramov: At some point they worked together on a single lexicon because they wanted it to be more interoperable, so that subscriptions from one show up in the other, and so on.

00:44:15.090 --> 00:44:19.370
Dan Abramov: They agreed on the format for a publication or a follow.

00:44:20.590 --> 00:44:21.230
Dan Abramov: This is called **standard.site**.

00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:30.090
Dan Abramov: It's become a bigger thing than this. It's kind of like RSS on ATProto in a sense.

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:47.250
Dan Abramov: The funny thing is now you can also adopt standard.site for your own blog that's not hosted on any of these platforms, and create records describing your post, your publication, and your brand colors in the publication.

00:44:47.250 --> 00:45:03.450
Dan Abramov: And links to any standard.site posts in the Bluesky app will be shown with a special treatment, with a box that has the branding color of your site.

00:45:03.710 --> 00:45:14.890
Dan Abramov: So this is an example where three different blogging platforms decided to share a lexicon, and then the Bluesky app decided to read that lexicon and use the information from it.

00:45:15.410 --> 00:45:23.110
Dan Abramov: In that sense, it's a much more dynamic ecosystem where some things get standardized, in a sense, but completely community-driven.

00:45:24.830 --> 00:45:25.750
Theodor Steiner: This is kind of wild.

00:45:25.950 --> 00:45:33.390
Theodor Steiner: This is like I built my own app, and to kickstart it, I let my users post from my app to Instagram.

00:45:34.250 --> 00:45:39.610
Theodor Steiner: I let them post pictures to Instagram, and I can also display every picture they posted to Instagram.

00:45:40.730 --> 00:45:43.790
Theodor Steiner: Yeah, this is the moment where it kind of clicks.

00:45:43.790 --> 00:45:49.210
Dan Abramov: Yeah. Well, the other thing is you can index things of different types, right?

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:55.330
Dan Abramov: Of course, indexing all Bluesky posts is crazy in terms of volume, so that's expensive.

00:45:55.330 --> 00:45:58.690
Dan Abramov: But you could maybe index something that relates to the user.

00:45:58.690 --> 00:46:01.370
Dan Abramov: And then you can do these kinds of mashups.

00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:04.270
Dan Abramov: There's another funny example.

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:09.650
Dan Abramov: There's this lexicon—do you know Last.fm, the music tracking thing?

00:46:09.670 --> 00:46:09.750
Theodor Steiner: Yeah, I've heard of that.

00:46:09.770 --> 00:46:10.090
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:14.530
Dan Abramov: So there's a lexicon called **Teal FM play**.

00:46:15.730 --> 00:46:27.470
Dan Abramov: Teal FM was supposed to be something where you track your music usage, but I don't think they've launched yet. It's been in development for a long time, but they're doing it on weekends.

00:46:28.350 --> 00:46:31.950
Dan Abramov: So they still haven't launched the actual website or the app.

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:34.790
Dan Abramov: But it doesn't matter because the lexicon exists.

00:46:35.630 --> 00:46:41.650
Dan Abramov: They already made tooling to submit what you're listening to, even though there is no app.

00:46:42.610 --> 00:46:44.390
Dan Abramov: You're just writing records of this lexicon.

00:46:45.650 --> 00:46:55.210
Dan Abramov: And so people have already made third-party apps that display your listening stats on Teal FM, even though Teal FM does not exist.

00:46:56.410 --> 00:46:57.290
Dan Abramov: It's kind of funny.

00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:03.690
Dan Abramov: You can show aggregations across it, like top listened artists on Teal FM.

00:47:04.050 --> 00:47:04.910
Dan Abramov: But again, there's no Teal FM.

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:16.110
Dan Abramov: The way I think about it is: if you had the world's JSON, why not run joins over products?

00:47:16.990 --> 00:47:23.650
Dan Abramov: You can do things like top tracks that your follows on Bluesky have been listening to.

00:47:24.430 --> 00:47:30.830
Dan Abramov: Or you can ask: what are the trending repositories from your friends?

00:47:31.510 --> 00:47:35.650
Dan Abramov: You can combine data from different apps and cross-join it.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:38.190
Dan Abramov: That feels like the web to me.

00:47:39.090 --> 00:47:42.150
Dan Abramov: It's taking the web idea and turning it into a web database.

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:42.750
Dan Abramov: I don't know.

00:47:42.910 --> 00:47:55.910
Dan Abramov: It's breaking the boundaries, or blurring the boundaries, between apps, and making it more like there's the web of data, and the apps are just lenses through which we look at the data.

00:47:55.910 --> 00:47:59.210
Dan Abramov: And these lenses can be... anyone can create a new one.

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:17.690
Theodor Steiner: And posting records across products sounds kind of crazy at first, but since it's the AT Protocol—Authenticated Transfer—that means you have authenticated everything that is posted to your repo, and that's why it's safe to post across apps, right?

00:48:18.310 --> 00:48:18.630
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:48:18.650 --> 00:48:28.090
Dan Abramov: So the way this works is apps can be sure that the content is really yours because they can always resolve it through the AT Protocol.

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:32.710
Dan Abramov: They go to your hosting, and your hosting returns them the thing.

00:48:33.490 --> 00:48:42.130
Dan Abramov: It is signed with the key that they can verify is the key in your DID document.

00:48:43.630 --> 00:48:49.790
Dan Abramov: And then the other clever thing they do is that you need to somehow scale up the network.

00:48:50.250 --> 00:48:55.270
Dan Abramov: You can imagine for each user there is hosting.

00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:58.070
Dan Abramov: Of course, a lot of users are going to be on the same hosting.

00:48:59.010 --> 00:49:07.630
Dan Abramov: Hosting can range from hosting 100,000 users to maybe a solo user hosting. Both of these are valid.

00:49:08.090 --> 00:49:09.750
Dan Abramov: But that's where the data lives.

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:12.790
Dan Abramov: Then you somehow need it to flow into all the apps, right?

00:49:13.270 --> 00:49:15.210
Dan Abramov: And it's inconvenient for apps to...

00:49:15.710 --> 00:49:22.090
Dan Abramov: You don't want them to smash all the hosting all the time to figure out what happened.

00:49:22.830 --> 00:49:25.210
Dan Abramov: So apps maintain caches and subscribe through WebSocket.

00:49:25.650 --> 00:49:28.230
Dan Abramov: But then there's a question of: how do you scale it further?

00:49:32.790 --> 00:49:33.550
Dan Abramov: You want some shared infrastructure.

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:36.530
Dan Abramov: You want shared caches. You want shared retransmission.

00:49:37.550 --> 00:49:56.170
Dan Abramov: Ideally, there should be a way to just subscribe over WebSocket to one event bus that has information about everything that happens on the network, or a subset that's relevant to your app—maybe your app's collection types and so on.

00:49:57.290 --> 00:50:01.910
Dan Abramov: The clever thing they did is that you can build retransmitters like this.

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:03.170
Dan Abramov: They're called relays.

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:10.770
Dan Abramov: Bluesky runs one. There are a bunch of community-run relays that let you subscribe over WebSocket to everything that happens on the network.

00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:14.810
Dan Abramov: The stream that is passed is a stream of commits.

00:50:15.330 --> 00:50:19.390
Dan Abramov: It's a stream of events about commits, identity changes, and so on.

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:21.610
Dan Abramov: And the stream is transmitted with proofs.

00:50:23.610 --> 00:50:29.370
Dan Abramov: The stream contains signed commits.

00:50:30.050 --> 00:50:31.690
Dan Abramov: So each commit is signed.

00:50:32.170 --> 00:50:34.350
Dan Abramov: You actually don't need to trust the retransmitter.

00:50:35.210 --> 00:50:36.630
Dan Abramov: That's another big idea.

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:43.210
Dan Abramov: Because cryptography is woven into it, you don't need to trust it.

00:50:43.690 --> 00:50:54.550
Dan Abramov: You can build community retransmitters and these nodes, and not have to worry that they're modifying the content as it flows through them.

00:50:55.270 --> 00:50:57.810
Dan Abramov: So that's the authenticated part in it.

00:50:58.110 --> 00:51:00.250
Theodor Steiner: So if I try to recap this for my understanding:

00:51:00.570 --> 00:51:04.930
Theodor Steiner: You have a very weird distribution of Personal Data Servers.

00:51:04.930 --> 00:51:12.010
Theodor Steiner: There are some with millions of users—probably the Bluesky ones—and then there are people who host it in their basement.

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:22.410
Theodor Steiner: And to not have every app know every PDS, go there every time, and pull them all the time, you have those relay services.

00:51:23.110 --> 00:51:30.390
Theodor Steiner: I imagine it's a super beefy server somewhere that somehow knows all PDSs and subscribes to all of them.

00:51:30.610 --> 00:51:36.330
Theodor Steiner: Then it's just like a huge Apache Kafka, and they pipe the events into the web.

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:36.670
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:51:36.690 --> 00:51:38.850
Dan Abramov: I don't think it needs to be super beefy.

00:51:38.990 --> 00:51:46.850
Dan Abramov: I'm not sure, but in terms of cost, I think it's something around 30 bucks a month to run it at the current volume.

00:51:47.430 --> 00:51:54.310
Dan Abramov: But yeah, they need to know the PDSs on the network, which is a bit wonky because how do you know?

00:51:54.930 --> 00:51:55.450
Dan Abramov: There are a bunch of ways.

00:51:56.230 --> 00:51:58.930
Dan Abramov: One is that you can enumerate them.

00:51:59.470 --> 00:52:03.270
Dan Abramov: I think there is an endpoint on existing relays to enumerate the ones that they know.

00:52:03.990 --> 00:52:05.890
Dan Abramov: That's how you can bootstrap.

00:52:06.870 --> 00:52:08.670
Dan Abramov: A PDS can also request to be crawled.

00:52:09.230 --> 00:52:14.850
Dan Abramov: So if you put a new PDS online, you can add some known relays to ping them.

00:52:15.530 --> 00:52:17.450
Dan Abramov: And then it can also discover it through links.

00:52:18.110 --> 00:52:25.430
Dan Abramov: If it sees a link to a PDS it hasn't seen before, and that resolves to a PDS it hasn't seen before, it can decide to crawl it.

00:52:26.490 --> 00:52:27.970
Dan Abramov: So it's kind of up to the relay how to do it.

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:39.130
Dan Abramov: And if it wants to just provide an archive or only retransmit and have no archive—because keeping an archive is kind of expensive...

00:52:39.970 --> 00:52:51.270
Theodor Steiner: So if I want to build on ATProto, I have this shared data layer, and I have apps that are already quite successful that populate that data layer with users.

00:52:51.670 --> 00:52:56.390
Theodor Steiner: Those users use those apps and therefore populate it with their own records.

00:52:56.390 --> 00:53:09.010
Theodor Steiner: And if I want to build on that, I would probably get one of those relays and start downloading data from there, putting it into my own database.

00:53:09.350 --> 00:53:10.170
Theodor Steiner: Is that how it works?

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:12.770
Theodor Steiner: And then I run queries and my app logic on that?

00:53:13.350 --> 00:53:13.800
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:53:13.870 --> 00:53:21.270
Dan Abramov: So I think the exact way you do it depends on what data your app is going to care about.

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:36.370
Dan Abramov: If it only cares about its own data—if you came up with new record types and that's the stuff that your app displays—then you don't need to backfill it because it doesn't exist.

00:53:36.730 --> 00:53:50.230
Dan Abramov: So if you just connect and start listening to a relay, and you filter it down to the stuff that your app cares about, then once records start getting created, you will have all of them.

00:53:50.770 --> 00:53:53.910
Dan Abramov: You don't actually need to get anything that existed before.

00:53:54.490 --> 00:53:55.570
Dan Abramov: That's the easy path.

00:53:56.410 --> 00:54:02.790
Dan Abramov: Then you just have a connection to this thing, and you keep track of where your users are.

00:54:02.790 --> 00:54:08.850
Dan Abramov: If you want to do something with existing data, then you have to backfill it.

00:54:12.310 --> 00:54:14.070
Dan Abramov: There are really two ways to go about it.

00:54:14.590 --> 00:54:24.070
Dan Abramov: The way I'm talking about is: if you want data to be in your own database, then of course you have to somehow get it there.

00:54:24.530 --> 00:54:28.550
Dan Abramov: Then the question is: is it from a big app or a small app?

00:54:28.850 --> 00:54:38.810
Dan Abramov: Because if it's something like Tangled, you can probably backfill all records from all Tangled users in a reasonable time because there's just not that much.

00:54:39.330 --> 00:54:45.750
Dan Abramov: Maybe 50,000 records. I don't know how many, but it's not that much.

00:54:46.390 --> 00:54:46.710
Dan Abramov: Or Teal FM.

00:54:46.730 --> 00:54:50.890
Dan Abramov: Okay, maybe a lot of records because people do scrobble. It's probably millions now.

00:54:51.270 --> 00:54:53.950
Dan Abramov: But essentially, you just need to find all the...

00:54:54.290 --> 00:55:04.830
Dan Abramov: I think maybe the relay has an API for this, but there's a way to find out which users or identities have a collection of a certain type.

00:55:05.210 --> 00:55:11.170
Dan Abramov: So you can figure out who the users of a particular app are and then just get their stuff.

00:55:12.010 --> 00:55:13.130
Dan Abramov: There are tools that do this.

00:55:13.190 --> 00:55:16.750
Dan Abramov: There's something called **TAP**, which is their tool for backfill.

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:23.490
Dan Abramov: You tell it which collections to look for, and it will try to synchronize it all and then listen to new events.

00:55:23.990 --> 00:55:25.770
Dan Abramov: But there's also a different approach.

00:55:27.250 --> 00:55:34.230
Dan Abramov: Especially if you want Bluesky, you probably don't want to download terabytes of all Bluesky posts that ever existed.

00:55:34.730 --> 00:55:37.490
Dan Abramov: But maybe you want to build some app on top of Bluesky.

00:55:37.490 --> 00:55:38.590
Dan Abramov: Of course, you can use the Bluesky API.

00:55:38.970 --> 00:55:45.850
Dan Abramov: That's the traditional way, but I think it's a bit not ATProto-spirited, right?

00:55:46.830 --> 00:55:48.490
Dan Abramov: You rely on somebody else's app-specific thing.

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:53.890
Dan Abramov: But there's a tool called **Constellation**.

00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:54.660
Dan Abramov: It's community-run.

00:55:55.790 --> 00:56:05.190
Dan Abramov: It's a server run by the community that indexes all the records and provides an API for backlinks.

00:56:05.950 --> 00:56:11.050
Dan Abramov: So it lets you say, given this post, give me all records that link to it.

00:56:12.310 --> 00:56:19.510
Dan Abramov: That means you can get all the likes of a post, all followers of a person, all quotes.

00:56:20.210 --> 00:56:25.930
Dan Abramov: It's kind of like going through the primary-foreign-key query.

00:56:26.070 --> 00:56:28.830
Dan Abramov: You ask: what are all the things that link to this piece of JSON?

00:56:29.710 --> 00:56:48.450
Dan Abramov: This actually lets you build a workable app without a backend, because you can hit the user's hosting if you need something user-specific, and then you can hit Constellation if you need to go one step removed and see all the things that link to this.

00:56:48.810 --> 00:56:51.350
Dan Abramov: This is less efficient, but you don't need a backend at all.

00:56:51.970 --> 00:56:52.420
Theodor Steiner: Yeah.

00:56:52.570 --> 00:56:59.130
Theodor Steiner: So the more I hear about this, at first I thought AT Protocol was really complicated.

00:56:59.530 --> 00:57:04.370
Theodor Steiner: But now I understand why it needs to be complicated, because it collapses the stack.

00:57:05.050 --> 00:57:06.610
Theodor Steiner: My app needs less and less.

00:57:06.730 --> 00:57:18.310
Theodor Steiner: It doesn't need a data layer necessarily, because every user has their own data layer, and I can use the existing relays and everything to aggregate that.

00:57:18.670 --> 00:57:26.010
Theodor Steiner: Then it doesn't need an authentication layer, because the user has—is it called an atmosphere account?

00:57:26.690 --> 00:57:26.850
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:57:26.870 --> 00:57:31.850
Dan Abramov: So it's kind of funny because it uses OAuth, right?

00:57:32.930 --> 00:57:36.870
Dan Abramov: You log in through OAuth, but the OAuth provider is actually your PDS.

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:41.250
Dan Abramov: So in a sense, it's almost like logging into a Google account, except you are Google.

00:57:42.290 --> 00:57:43.050
Dan Abramov: It's your hosting.

00:57:44.710 --> 00:57:54.690
Dan Abramov: This is why, if you change to **eurosky.social**, when you click "login through atmosphere" in other apps, it's going to show the eurosky.social login page now, because that is now your hosting.

00:57:55.070 --> 00:57:57.150
Theodor Steiner: Yeah, the domain is eurosky.social, and that's where I log in.

00:57:57.650 --> 00:57:58.100
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:57:58.310 --> 00:57:59.210
Theodor Steiner: Super interesting.

00:57:59.910 --> 00:58:00.230
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:58:00.250 --> 00:58:02.290
Dan Abramov: So it's lifting identity and the data.

00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:06.490
Dan Abramov: I think of it as the people-centric data layer for the web.

00:58:06.950 --> 00:58:10.990
Theodor Steiner: That also brings its own complexity though, right?

00:58:11.430 --> 00:58:16.030
Theodor Steiner: For example, let's just take an Instagram competitor.

00:58:16.370 --> 00:58:21.850
Theodor Steiner: You want to build that app, and it's slow, and you want to debug or improve performance.

00:58:21.850 --> 00:58:24.930
Theodor Steiner: But the problem could be outside of your product.

00:58:24.930 --> 00:58:28.730
Theodor Steiner: It could be the user's PDS being really slow or laggy.

00:58:29.150 --> 00:58:31.650
Theodor Steiner: It could be the relay struggling to aggregate the data.

00:58:32.210 --> 00:58:33.970
Theodor Steiner: How do people cope with that?

00:58:34.590 --> 00:58:34.910
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

00:58:34.910 --> 00:58:41.670
Dan Abramov: I think with hosting itself, the idea is pretty resilient to that.

00:58:44.290 --> 00:58:53.070
Dan Abramov: If you want a good app, you just shouldn't really go to hosting much, because you aggregate things as they come in.

00:58:53.790 --> 00:58:55.710
Dan Abramov: So the hosting just emits events.

00:58:56.510 --> 00:58:58.990
Dan Abramov: You shouldn't blast the hosting with a thousand requests.

00:58:59.170 --> 00:59:03.710
Dan Abramov: You serve everything from your database or from the stuff that you've cached.

00:59:04.330 --> 00:59:11.710
Dan Abramov: So hosting being a bit wonky is actually fine, as long as it actually emits the events that it should generate.

00:59:12.410 --> 00:59:14.570
Dan Abramov: That isn't really a major problem.

00:59:14.970 --> 00:59:22.010
Dan Abramov: It's just a server that serves some JSON, so it shouldn't really get laggy.

00:59:22.270 --> 00:59:33.030
Dan Abramov: Of course, for users who are on Bluesky, if Bluesky is having scaling issues, then that does reflect in the app.

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:35.670
Dan Abramov: Some events might struggle to come through.

00:59:36.402 --> 00:59:38.598
Dan Abramov: The relay might struggle.

00:59:39.330 --> 00:59:41.170
Dan Abramov: This is something you just kind of live with.

00:59:43.570 --> 00:59:50.490
Dan Abramov: Your app is more webby, so there are more things that could go wrong.

00:59:50.490 --> 00:59:58.710
Dan Abramov: The relay itself—again, if you rely on the one run by Bluesky, it's mostly working fine.

00:59:58.710 --> 01:00:02.110
Dan Abramov: I think there have been incidents, but it's mostly working fine.

01:00:02.750 --> 01:00:11.650
Dan Abramov: If you don't trust it, you can definitely run your own, and that is how you completely isolate yourself from problems upstream.

01:00:15.490 --> 01:00:26.410
Dan Abramov: There were also cases where other apps that run their own relays or used the community relay were up when the Bluesky app was not working, because there was some relay problem.

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:28.110
Dan Abramov: I think with time...

01:00:29.490 --> 01:00:40.030
Dan Abramov: Right now it's the early adopter stage, where you run it because you believe there is a better way to do web products.

01:00:40.990 --> 01:00:42.810
Dan Abramov: I think of it as open source in the 90s.

01:00:45.330 --> 01:00:56.170
Dan Abramov: People would tell you you're crazy, or Microsoft says it's cancer, and then 10 years later Microsoft is actually running on open source.

01:00:56.530 --> 01:01:00.410
Dan Abramov: So I think right now we're at that stage where people tell you you're crazy.

01:01:03.590 --> 01:01:05.110
Dan Abramov: These are early adopter pains.

01:01:05.630 --> 01:01:16.610
Dan Abramov: But because it's a standard, everything that benefits some apps can benefit others because they speak the same language.

01:01:17.410 --> 01:01:25.290
Dan Abramov: There's shared infrastructure, shared libraries, shared tools, different kinds of things you can do with it.

01:01:26.610 --> 01:01:34.310
Dan Abramov: As that infrastructure grows and becomes more reusable, the network will become more resilient in general.

01:01:34.930 --> 01:01:36.610
Dan Abramov: There will be more relay options.

01:01:40.070 --> 01:01:41.310
Dan Abramov: It'll be easier to backfill.

01:01:41.730 --> 01:01:47.470
Dan Abramov: There will be archives for rehydrating everything if you lose some data.

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:50.030
Dan Abramov: So I think we'll see more of that.

01:01:50.510 --> 01:01:50.970
Theodor Steiner: Yeah.

01:01:51.030 --> 01:01:56.730
Theodor Steiner: I actually just thought of a really interesting problem concerning sustainability.

01:01:57.410 --> 01:02:02.890
Theodor Steiner: You talked about Bluesky being both the app layer and the PDS layer.

01:02:04.250 --> 01:02:09.830
Theodor Steiner: For Bluesky, I can clearly see a path to monetization.

01:02:10.070 --> 01:02:13.310
Theodor Steiner: They have the app layer, and it would be quite easy for them to monetize that.

01:02:13.650 --> 01:02:16.650
Theodor Steiner: Let's say display ads or premium accounts.

01:02:17.330 --> 01:02:28.350
Theodor Steiner: But there are PDSs that don't have an app layer, and I'd say they bear most of the cost because they have the egress and hosting costs.

01:02:30.230 --> 01:02:41.510
Theodor Steiner: Is there a way for apps to give back to the PDSs, or somehow shift the weight of that cost structure?

01:02:42.130 --> 01:02:42.530
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

01:02:42.550 --> 01:02:43.730
Dan Abramov: I'm not sure.

01:02:44.470 --> 01:02:50.790
Dan Abramov: I think running an app is many times more expensive than running hosting.

01:02:51.870 --> 01:03:04.310
Dan Abramov: I'm not 100% sure, but first, the app serves the users.

01:03:05.590 --> 01:03:10.890
Dan Abramov: If you have a million daily users, that's the load that goes into the app.

01:03:11.910 --> 01:03:13.730
Dan Abramov: The hosting serves the apps.

01:03:14.090 --> 01:03:17.530
Dan Abramov: So that's actually much more distributed already.

01:03:18.850 --> 01:03:20.450
Dan Abramov: Especially if there are different hosts.

01:03:21.170 --> 01:03:23.850
Dan Abramov: If you self-host, you really only serve your own data.

01:03:28.030 --> 01:03:35.950
Dan Abramov: Compared to something like Mastodon, where if your post goes viral, all the instances are going to hammer your instance.

01:03:37.790 --> 01:03:42.570
Dan Abramov: Here it doesn't really matter that much because the app shields you.

01:03:43.610 --> 01:03:52.030
Dan Abramov: The other thing is that I think hosting is still such a novel concept.

01:03:52.470 --> 01:03:59.890
Dan Abramov: Usually you have hosting if you have a website or a company.

01:04:00.230 --> 01:04:09.570
Dan Abramov: But as part of your internet experience, even if you log in with a Google account, that's kind of the relationship you have to Google.

01:04:10.970 --> 01:04:14.310
Dan Abramov: The idea is that we can lift this up and make it transferable.

01:04:14.510 --> 01:04:16.370
Dan Abramov: I think it's kind of new.

01:04:16.950 --> 01:04:20.090
Dan Abramov: There's a Dropbox with all your social data.

01:04:20.630 --> 01:04:21.650
Dan Abramov: That's kind of a new idea.

01:04:22.290 --> 01:04:27.190
Dan Abramov: I don't think it's even recognized by mainstream users at this moment.

01:04:27.390 --> 01:04:30.270
Dan Abramov: Maybe in five years, or maybe in ten years, it will be.

01:04:30.910 --> 01:04:33.630
Dan Abramov: But hosting can also offer services.

01:04:37.070 --> 01:04:39.630
Dan Abramov: I'd actually pay for safe backups.

01:04:41.590 --> 01:04:46.730
Dan Abramov: I'd really want my hosting provider to periodically back up all my data without me thinking about it.

01:04:46.870 --> 01:04:48.670
Dan Abramov: That could be one thing.

01:04:48.670 --> 01:04:53.530
Dan Abramov: Some people might want more storage, maybe for videos and things like that.

01:04:55.310 --> 01:04:59.050
Dan Abramov: But in general, I think it's actually the app that's much more expensive.

01:05:00.870 --> 01:05:11.650
Dan Abramov: Things like CDNs, video transcoding, serving lots of video—all of that is way more expensive than serving JSON.

01:05:13.150 --> 01:05:15.130
Dan Abramov: That's where I think a lot of the costs actually go.

01:05:15.130 --> 01:05:23.830
Theodor Steiner: So you're saying that in order to run a stable app, you want to shield the PDSs from your app layer anyway.

01:05:24.710 --> 01:05:31.010
Theodor Steiner: And shielding the PDSs will take most of the load off the PDSs and onto your app layer.

01:05:31.170 --> 01:05:34.490
Theodor Steiner: So it's sort of a self-restraining system.

01:05:35.170 --> 01:05:35.620
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

01:05:36.210 --> 01:05:38.530
Dan Abramov: Because you want it to be reliable.

01:05:40.030 --> 01:05:45.130
Dan Abramov: If a PDS is misbehaving, you don't want that to reflect in the app.

01:05:48.110 --> 01:05:51.810
Dan Abramov: Past a certain level of product complexity, you just have to have a database.

01:05:51.810 --> 01:05:56.030
Dan Abramov: Or you can hit community caches, but they're shielding you.

01:05:57.010 --> 01:06:06.090
Dan Abramov: If you hit Constellation, that also shields hosting because Constellation has a cache of everything that it has indexed.

01:06:06.090 --> 01:06:06.090
Theodor Steiner: Interesting.

01:06:06.090 --> 01:06:12.110
Theodor Steiner: So what would you say is currently still missing from the AT Protocol?

01:06:12.110 --> 01:06:14.650
Theodor Steiner: Is there something that's not here yet?

01:06:15.230 --> 01:06:15.680
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

01:06:16.230 --> 01:06:23.870
Dan Abramov: There are a lot of rough edges in terms of developer experience, or what the right way to do something is.

01:06:27.630 --> 01:06:28.810
Dan Abramov: People are still experimenting.

01:06:30.370 --> 01:06:38.170
Dan Abramov: But I think the big thing the team is currently working on is permissioned data.

01:06:40.210 --> 01:06:44.850
Dan Abramov: Right now, ATProto is fully focused on public data, which I think was the right focus.

01:06:47.690 --> 01:06:50.590
Dan Abramov: If you start with private data, that's a much smaller scale.

01:06:50.850 --> 01:06:52.810
Dan Abramov: You don't know that you're going to scale up to public.

01:06:53.330 --> 01:07:01.170
Dan Abramov: Whereas if you scale up to public, you have to figure out aggregation and all these things efficiently.

01:07:01.930 --> 01:07:03.870
Dan Abramov: Then you can scale down to permissioned data.

01:07:05.610 --> 01:07:15.550
Dan Abramov: So they're working on extending the protocol—or maybe having a sibling protocol—for stuff that isn't meant for public retransmission.

01:07:17.670 --> 01:07:18.370
Dan Abramov: Things like groups.

01:07:19.010 --> 01:07:24.910
Dan Abramov: Not messaging, because for that you really want end-to-end encryption, and that's an entirely separate thing.

01:07:25.250 --> 01:07:29.370
Dan Abramov: You could still use part of the identity layer, but it's a separate problem.

01:07:29.810 --> 01:07:42.310
Dan Abramov: Whereas things that are gated by membership aren't completely private, because any app that has been granted access still needs to see them in order to display them.

01:07:42.870 --> 01:07:47.710
Dan Abramov: So they're working on this permissioned-data extension to the protocol.

01:07:48.630 --> 01:07:57.390
Dan Abramov: Right now, if you want something private or group-private, you can do it with your own database.

01:07:59.050 --> 01:08:00.290
Dan Abramov: Which is also okay.

01:08:00.910 --> 01:08:05.590
Dan Abramov: ATProto is pragmatic in the sense that you can adopt just the parts that make sense to you.

01:08:05.730 --> 01:08:09.650
Dan Abramov: You can use only the identity layer and keep everything else in your own database.

01:08:11.830 --> 01:08:16.190
Dan Abramov: Ideally you'd move towards "ATProto-ing" it a bit more.

01:08:16.370 --> 01:08:24.470
Dan Abramov: But then you run into the wall where group-level stuff, user preferences, and things like that have to live somewhere.

01:08:25.870 --> 01:08:29.670
Dan Abramov: There's a hacky thing where I think you can store some of it in Bluesky preferences.

01:08:29.670 --> 01:08:33.890
Dan Abramov: But anyway, there's no good API for private or protected data.

01:08:35.230 --> 01:08:36.190
Dan Abramov: That's what they're working on.

01:08:36.190 --> 01:08:38.050
Dan Abramov: I think that's the biggest gap.

01:08:40.190 --> 01:08:40.950
Dan Abramov: There's currently no protocol-native way to do that.

01:08:42.710 --> 01:08:48.450
Theodor Steiner: So this might be a sacrilege in a podcast with a React core member, but I'm a huge fan of Svelte.

01:08:49.390 --> 01:08:53.070
Theodor Steiner: In the ATProto world, quite a few apps seem to be built with Svelte.

01:08:53.750 --> 01:09:00.090
Theodor Steiner: There's someone—or maybe a group of people—trying to build a Discord clone based on ATProto.

01:09:00.470 --> 01:09:02.090
Theodor Steiner: I think it's called Roomy Chat or something.

01:09:02.090 --> 01:09:02.090
Dan Abramov: Yeah. Yes.

01:09:02.090 --> 01:09:08.990
Theodor Steiner: And they seem to be stuck on having private servers because they don't have permissioned data yet.

01:09:09.610 --> 01:09:17.130
Theodor Steiner: Having permissioned data would unlock that for them because they could have data being created behind access controls.

01:09:18.290 --> 01:09:19.310
Dan Abramov: Yeah, probably.

01:09:19.550 --> 01:09:20.030
Dan Abramov: I think so.

01:09:20.550 --> 01:09:26.890
Dan Abramov: Things like chats are just a different modality because they're kind of ephemeral.

01:09:27.950 --> 01:09:35.330
Dan Abramov: Maybe for Discord you do want to keep it, but I don't know that you necessarily want every chat message to be a record.

01:09:35.890 --> 01:09:37.190
Dan Abramov: I don't know if that makes sense.

01:09:37.710 --> 01:09:41.030
Dan Abramov: But having permissioned data would definitely help.

01:09:42.430 --> 01:09:44.650
Dan Abramov: Svelte is really popular in that part of the community.

01:09:45.650 --> 01:09:46.100
Dan Abramov: I'm not sure why.

01:09:46.730 --> 01:09:48.690
Dan Abramov: Maybe it just fits the ethos.

01:09:49.330 --> 01:09:49.780
Dan Abramov: It's kind of webby.

01:09:51.470 --> 01:09:58.230
Dan Abramov: It has the connotation of being the lighter thing, the newer thing.

01:09:58.230 --> 01:10:00.910
Dan Abramov: And React is like Java to these people.

01:10:00.910 --> 01:10:02.750
Dan Abramov: Which is okay.

01:10:03.490 --> 01:10:04.810
Dan Abramov: I'll take it.

01:10:04.810 --> 01:10:07.270
Dan Abramov: Whatever works.

01:10:07.930 --> 01:10:15.750
Theodor Steiner: It might just be a function of Svelte adopters being early adopters, and then ATProto also being quite early.

01:10:16.270 --> 01:10:16.830
Dan Abramov: Maybe. Yeah.

01:10:17.290 --> 01:10:18.750
Dan Abramov: But I think a lot of it is aesthetic.

01:10:19.730 --> 01:10:24.010
Dan Abramov: It feels webby, and ATProto is also very webby.

01:10:24.210 --> 01:10:26.570
Dan Abramov: So I think there's a match there.

01:10:27.930 --> 01:10:31.710
Theodor Steiner: Okay, I think we covered so much ground in this episode.

01:10:31.850 --> 01:10:34.510
Theodor Steiner: Is there anything left to say?

01:10:34.770 --> 01:10:36.250
Theodor Steiner: Anything that we haven't covered yet?

01:10:36.250 --> 01:10:39.750
Dan Abramov: I don't know.

01:10:39.790 --> 01:10:46.590
Dan Abramov: I would encourage more people to actually try Bluesky for real—for posting the stuff you would normally post on Twitter.

01:10:47.630 --> 01:10:50.230
Dan Abramov: One thing I would encourage is to actually...

01:10:50.430 --> 01:10:55.170
Dan Abramov: The default feed, I think, is called Discover, and I don't think it's very good.

01:10:55.750 --> 01:11:01.810
Dan Abramov: There is a very good custom feed called **For You**, literally like the "For You" feed on Twitter.

01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:08.010
Dan Abramov: If you go to the Feeds tab in the Bluesky app and search for it, you'll find it.

01:11:08.410 --> 01:11:09.930
Dan Abramov: I highly recommend it.

01:11:10.010 --> 01:11:16.770
Dan Abramov: It learns from your likes and from what people who liked the same things as you have also liked.

01:11:19.370 --> 01:11:23.490
Dan Abramov: I honestly feel it's about as good as the Twitter algorithm used to be.

01:11:24.730 --> 01:11:25.690
Dan Abramov: I always use that feed.

01:11:26.510 --> 01:11:32.330
Dan Abramov: The other funny thing is that this feed literally runs from somebody's gaming computer at home.

01:11:34.190 --> 01:11:36.450
Dan Abramov: Again, because what is a feed?

01:11:36.450 --> 01:11:44.430
Dan Abramov: It's just an HTTP service that returns an array of AT URIs for posts.

01:11:47.310 --> 01:11:51.450
Dan Abramov: Everything in the network is identified by an AT URI, so it doesn't matter where it's hosted.

01:11:51.450 --> 01:11:57.730
Dan Abramov: You can build these services because they're all talking about the same thing.

01:11:59.170 --> 01:12:13.050
Dan Abramov: This For You feed just returns an array of AT URIs, paginates through them, and then the Bluesky app talks to that feed, gets the URIs, and hydrates them from the database into actual posts.

01:12:13.790 --> 01:12:17.630
Dan Abramov: It's very cool that you can have userland feeds on Bluesky.

01:12:18.750 --> 01:12:19.690
Dan Abramov: I think this is the best one.

01:12:21.490 --> 01:12:29.450
Dan Abramov: Especially if you're trying to get people to move over and try Bluesky, just tell them to install the For You feed because it's simply better.

01:12:29.990 --> 01:12:31.190
Theodor Steiner: I'm also on For You.

01:12:31.270 --> 01:12:43.570
Theodor Steiner: And it's super funny because sometimes the person who hosts the feed goes down for an hour, and then it comes back up and he posts something like, "Sorry, I had to upgrade my PC. I put more RAM into it."

01:12:44.250 --> 01:12:44.570
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

01:12:44.590 --> 01:12:45.210
Theodor Steiner: It's very hacky-cool.

01:12:46.730 --> 01:12:47.180
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

01:12:47.490 --> 01:12:47.970
Theodor Steiner: Okay.

01:12:48.110 --> 01:12:50.850
Theodor Steiner: I think that's it for today then.

01:12:51.090 --> 01:12:51.310
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

01:12:51.330 --> 01:12:53.830
Theodor Steiner: Thank you so much for coming on and talking about ATProto.

01:12:55.050 --> 01:13:00.990
Theodor Steiner: I mean, you obviously couldn't say it yourself, but probably the best way to learn about ATProto is your blog.

01:13:02.090 --> 01:13:08.830
Theodor Steiner: You have a whole series of blog posts on ATProto and how it works, using different ways to break it down.

01:13:08.990 --> 01:13:10.830
Theodor Steiner: I think the file system one is great.

01:13:11.990 --> 01:13:15.730
Theodor Steiner: Then go on Bluesky and just start following a bunch of people.

01:13:15.910 --> 01:13:19.330
Theodor Steiner: My personal recommendation would be the Starter Packs.

01:13:20.970 --> 01:13:25.510
Theodor Steiner: They let you, with just one click, follow everyone in your area of interest.

01:13:25.897 --> 01:13:26.070
Dan Abramov: Yeah.

01:13:26.090 --> 01:13:30.470
Theodor Steiner: For example, I assume you'd be in the React Starter Pack, or the Web Dev Starter Pack.

01:13:31.030 --> 01:13:32.670
Dan Abramov: Yeah. Probably, if there is one.

01:13:33.080 --> 01:13:34.720
Dan Abramov: There's definitely one for JavaScript.

01:13:35.130 --> 01:13:42.170
Theodor Steiner: I'm in the Svelte Starter Pack, which was really cool because it got me bootstrapped with a whole bunch of likes, which I was really happy about.

01:13:42.557 --> 01:13:43.007
Dan Abramov: All right.

01:13:43.330 --> 01:13:51.870
Theodor Steiner: And actually, this show is also on Bluesky—or if it isn't yet, I'll make sure it will be after this post.

01:13:52.570 --> 01:13:56.390
Theodor Steiner: You can always check us on the regular HTTP web.

01:13:56.730 --> 01:13:57.410
Theodor Steiner: Our domain is **uit-inside.linecorp.com**.

01:14:02.070 --> 01:14:04.410
Theodor Steiner: Make sure to follow Dan if you don't already.

01:14:05.210 --> 01:14:06.730
Theodor Steiner: And thank you so much for listening.

01:14:07.210 --> 01:14:14.090
Theodor Steiner: If you want to listen to other episodes, most of them are in Japanese, but I think there are three or four English episodes out there.

01:14:14.150 --> 01:14:16.070
Theodor Steiner: So just look for them.

01:14:16.070 --> 01:14:17.470
Theodor Steiner: Or maybe study Japanese.

01:14:17.870 --> 01:14:19.310
Theodor Steiner: Or use a tool to translate them.

01:14:20.370 --> 01:14:21.830
Theodor Steiner: Thank you so much for coming on.

01:14:22.100 --> 01:14:22.350
Theodor Steiner: Bye-bye.

01:14:22.370 --> 01:14:22.820
Dan Abramov: Thank you.
